WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(restless music)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:05.000
- [Voiceover] This is a
Japanese giant salamander.

00:00:06.179 --> 00:00:07.346
It is massive.

00:00:12.935 --> 00:00:14.923
This primeval amphibian can grow up to

00:00:14.923 --> 00:00:18.244
five feet long and weigh over 50 pounds,

00:00:18.244 --> 00:00:20.150
making it one of the largest salamanders

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:23.150
to ever crawl the face of the Earth.

00:00:24.103 --> 00:00:25.804
Despite it's primitive look,

00:00:25.804 --> 00:00:28.913
the Japanese giant
salamander is highly evolved.

00:00:28.913 --> 00:00:31.579
It is covered head to tail
with specialized cells

00:00:31.579 --> 00:00:35.197
that detect tiny vibrations
in its environment.

00:00:35.197 --> 00:00:37.102
When it detects a threat, it excretes

00:00:37.102 --> 00:00:40.692
a strong-smelling ooze that
smells like a Japanese pepper,

00:00:40.692 --> 00:00:44.859
hence, its common Japanese
name, giant pepper fish.

00:00:45.776 --> 00:00:49.155
The Japanese giant salamander
is entirely aquatic,

00:00:49.155 --> 00:00:52.676
and spends its life in
clear, cool flowing streams.

00:00:52.676 --> 00:00:54.343
It has no gills, but instead

00:00:54.343 --> 00:00:56.540
absorbs oxygen through its skin.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:59.194
If left alone, the
Japanese giant salamander

00:00:59.194 --> 00:01:01.208
will live a long life.

00:01:01.208 --> 00:01:03.945
This one is 26 years old, and in the wild

00:01:03.945 --> 00:01:06.348
they can live to be nearly 80.

00:01:06.348 --> 00:01:09.487
But pollution of its river
habitat, and over collection,

00:01:09.487 --> 00:01:13.831
are threatening this
fascinating primordial creature.

00:01:13.831 --> 00:01:16.653
This is the Japanese giant salamander.

00:01:16.653 --> 00:01:19.403
(restless music)

